{
  "markdown_report": "At 0% progress, prioritize a small architectural change: **history-conditioned dilation gates inside NextItNet**. This is a testable hypothesis, with no claimed validation improvement.\n\n**Insights from the starting point.** (1) Users report stale interests and broadly relevant recommendations displacing specific recent matches. Context selection is therefore a plausible intervention. (2) Electronics has 12.27% sampled ranking errors and a −0.2595 tenth-percentile margin. These identify confusing training pairs, not validation performance. (3) Collapse scores of 0.5071–0.5234 imply mean cosine similarities near zero. Global embedding dispersion is consequently a weaker priority.\n\n**Insights from related work.** (1) NextItNet supplies causal residual convolutions with expanding receptive fields [1]. Learned scale selection could retain this backbone while adapting context length. (2) FADC demonstrates adaptive dilation in segmentation [2]. Its relevance is architectural; recommendation gains remain unestablished. (3) Sampled-softmax research motivates multiple competing negatives [3]. Temperature sensitivity cautions against immediately emphasizing the hardest examples.\n\n**Framework and directions.**\n\n| Direction | Existing ingredient | Proposed experiment | Main uncertainty |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Context selection | NextItNet; FADC | Position-dependent dilation gates | Whether context selection improves specific matches |\n| Ranking objective | Sampled softmax | Uniform multiple-negative training | False negatives and temperature |\n| Item identity | Text-enhanced recommendation | Jointly trained title encoder | Sparse text supervision |\n\n**Candidate assessment.** Scores are originality/potential/user alignment/model alignment/difficulty, each 0–10; higher difficulty means harder implementation. Each entry gives a positive and negative consideration.\n\n1. **Dilation gates: 5/7/8/6/4.** Originality: contextual adaptation / familiar mechanism. Potential: interpretable scale selection / gates may collapse. User alignment: targets stale interests and broad purpose mismatch / cannot establish compatibility. Model alignment: may resolve ambiguous contexts / separation gains are uncertain. Difficulty: local block change / padding requires care.\n2. **Multiple-negative softmax: 3/7/5/9/3.** Originality: useful adaptation / established objective. Potential: efficient competition / sampling sensitivity. User alignment: ranks alternatives / weak intent specificity. Model alignment: directly targets separation / sampled margins can mislead. Difficulty: small loss change / exclusion bookkeeping.\n3. **Trainable title encoder: 4/6/8/6/6.** Originality: constrained adaptation / familiar fusion. Potential: explicit item identity / limited supervision. User alignment: purpose cues / compatibility remains unknown. Model alignment: distinguishes confusing items / metadata noise. Difficulty: lightweight encoder / vocabulary and predictor changes.\n\n**Selected experiment.** Replace one residual block with jointly trained causal dilation branches and history-conditioned softmax gates. Small receptive fields can emphasize recent coherent behavior; larger fields preserve useful older interests. This explicitly targets stale-interest persistence and broad relevance displacing immediate-purpose matches. Its model-facing target is Electronics’ weak lower-tail separation, through more selective context representations.\n\nInitialize gates uniformly and compare with fixed uniform gates. Keep one network/checkpoint per category, all protocol limits, and final-nonpadding readout. Select exclusively by pinned macro validation HR@10; use measured diagnostics to test mechanisms. References: [1] NextItNet, https://arxiv.org/abs/1808.05163; [2] FADC, https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.05369; [3] sampled softmax, https://wujcan.github.io/papers/tois-ssm.pdf.",
  "idea": {
    "description": "Add history-conditioned gates over causal dilation scales inside one NextItNet residual block. All branches belong to one jointly trained network and produce one feature representation and one item score.",
    "motivation": "The user diagnosis identifies stale-interest persistence and broad relevance displacing specific recent matches. Adaptive receptive fields could address both by selecting context at each position. Electronics’ negative tenth-percentile margin supplies a model-facing motivation to improve context discrimination, although neither these diagnostics nor the related papers establish an HR@10 benefit.",
    "implementation_notes": "Start from the host’s corrected NextItNet baseline and replace only one residual block initially. Use causal branches with kernel size 3 and dilations 1, 2, and 4; retain the baseline residual and normalization conventions. Compute gate logits from causal hidden states and a causal mean of the last four valid states; initialize the final gate projection to zero for uniform initial weights. Mask padding after every branch and residual update, since biases can otherwise propagate padding features. Preserve the existing training loss initially to isolate the intervention. Keep log2feats(sequence), item_emb, predict_score(features, items), sample_batch and ModelView compatible. Final readout must gather the maximum index whose item ID is nonzero, with an explicit empty-history fallback. Update the embedded predictor writer and checkpoint configuration with identical branch and gate definitions. Retain DiagnosisProbe(view, device), run_full_diagnosis(train_loader), and diagnosis_interpreter_prompt(raw_diagnosis); return measured metrics and definitions, treating unavailable metadata breakdowns as unavailable. Gate entropy and scale weights may be added as descriptive diagnostics, without equating scales with verified intent. Preserve four-category routing, preprocessing seed 0, training seed 42, maximum 200 epochs, 200 batches per epoch, single-GPU execution, DATA_DIR = os.environ['GAGC_DATA_DIR'], and trial-local artifacts. Compare learned gates against uniform gates within the shared budget using only host validation HR@10. No independent scoring components or checkpoint averaging.",
    "pseudocode": "For each category, initialize one corrected NextItNet model with seed 42.\nFor each permitted training batch:\n    mask = (sequence != 0)\n    h = baseline input embedding and preceding causal blocks\n    context = causal_valid_mean(h, mask, window=4)\n    weights = softmax(gate(concat(h, context)), dim=scale)\n    branches = [causal_branch(h, dilation=d) * mask for d in [1,2,4]]\n    mixed = sum(weights[..., k:k+1] * branches[k] for k in range(3))\n    h = baseline_residual_update(h, mixed) * mask\n    features = remaining_baseline_blocks(h, mask)\n    loss = unchanged_baseline_loss(features, positive, negative)\n    update all network parameters jointly\nEvaluate using the pinned host validation protocol.\nSave one selected model state and its architecture configuration per category.\nAt prediction, reconstruct that architecture and gather features at the final nonpadding index.",
    "originality": {
      "score": 5,
      "positive": "History-conditioned dilation selection is a plausible adaptation of adaptive receptive fields to the supplied NextItNet baseline.",
      "negative": "Multiscale convolutions and learned gates are established ingredients; the supplied search does not justify claiming a novel mechanism."
    },
    "future_potential": {
      "score": 7,
      "positive": "A successful controlled experiment could motivate systematic study of context-length selection across recommendation domains.",
      "negative": "Gates may converge to fixed weights, and qualitative intent improvements require additional measured evidence."
    },
    "code_difficulty": {
      "score": 4,
      "positive": "The intervention is localized to a residual block and preserves the baseline training objective and scoring interface.",
      "negative": "Causal padding, intermediate masking, checkpoint configuration and predictor-writer parity require careful implementation."
    }
  },
  "related_work": [
    {
      "title": "NextItNet: A Simple Convolutional Generative Network for Next Item Recommendation",
      "link": "https://arxiv.org/abs/1808.05163",
      "contributions": [
        "Provides the causal residual dilated-convolution backbone retained by the proposed experiment.",
        "Expands receptive fields to capture sequential dependencies."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Does not establish benefits from the proposed history-conditioned scale gates.",
        "Does not establish practical compatibility or current purchase intent."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Frequency-Adaptive Dilated Convolution for Semantic Segmentation",
      "link": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.05369",
      "contributions": [
        "Provides a cross-domain precedent for adaptive dilation and frequency selection."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Evidence concerns semantic segmentation.",
        "Its results do not establish improvements for causal sequential recommendation."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "On the Effectiveness of Sampled Softmax Loss for Item Recommendation",
      "link": "https://wujcan.github.io/papers/tois-ssm.pdf",
      "contributions": [
        "Motivates efficient training with multiple competing negatives.",
        "Highlights temperature-sensitive behavior relevant to negative hardness."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Does not establish an Electronics-specific improvement.",
        "Changing the objective alongside architecture would complicate attribution in the initial experiment."
      ]
    }
  ]
}
